"<a>Open Stellarium Web</a> (free, runs in the browser) · <a>Stellarium for Windows, Mac and Linux</a> (free download, the same sky)",
"Use a planetarium program to find stars by two kinds of sky coordinates, see which stars never set from Ruston, and follow the Sun along the ecliptic through the year.",
"<strong>Altitude and azimuth.</strong> The simplest way to say where a star is: its altitude is its angle above the horizon ({#}° on the horizon, {#}° straight overhead), and its azimuth is its compass direction ({#}° north, {#}° east, {#}° south, {#}° west). Both change all night as Earth turns, and they are different for observers in different places.",
"<strong>Right ascension and declination.</strong> These are like longitude and latitude, but fixed on the sky. Declination is the angle north (+) or south (−) of the celestial equator, the line on the sky right above Earth’s equator. Right ascension is measured eastward around the sky in hours and minutes, from {#} h to {#} h. A star keeps the same right ascension and declination all night and all year (they change only very slowly, through precession), so star charts and catalogs use them.",
"<strong>Polaris and your latitude.</strong> Polaris lies within {#}° of the north celestial pole, the point in the sky right above Earth’s North Pole. Seen from latitude L north, the pole stands L degrees above the northern horizon. Ruston, Louisiana, is at latitude {#}° N.",
"<strong>Circumpolar stars.</strong> As the sky turns, every star circles the pole once a day. A star close enough to the pole never dips below the horizon: it is circumpolar. From latitude L north, a star is circumpolar if its declination is more than {#}° − L.",
"<strong>The ecliptic.</strong> As Earth orbits the Sun, the Sun appears to move eastward against the stars, once around the sky in a year, along a path called the ecliptic. The constellations it passes through are the constellations of the zodiac. The modern constellation boundaries were set by the International Astronomical Union in {#}.",
"Predict the altitude of Polaris as seen from Ruston.",
"Using the rule in the Background, what is the smallest declination a star can have and still be circumpolar from Ruston?",
"Stars rise in the east and set in the west. In the evening, does a star in the east climb higher or sink lower as the hours pass?",
"Predict how high the noon Sun gets at Ruston on June {#}, its highest day of the year. Does it pass straight overhead (altitude {#}°)? If not, about how far below overhead does it stay: a few degrees, about {#}°, or more than {#}°? Explain your choice.",
"No hazards. If you go outside to compare with the real sky, stay somewhere safe, and never look at the Sun.",
"Stellarium shows the sky for the place and time you set. To find a star, search for it by name (Stellarium for Windows, Mac and Linux: press F3; Stellarium Web: the search box at the top). Clicking a star or the Sun shows its information, including its azimuth and altitude (Az./Alt.) and its right ascension and declination (RA/Dec). Stellarium may give the RA/Dec for the year {#} (J2000) and for the present date; record the J2000 values if both are shown. Stellarium gives angles in degrees, minutes and seconds: write altitude, azimuth and declination in degrees to the nearest {#}° ({#}′ make {#}°, so +{#}°{#}′ is +{#}°), and right ascension in hours and minutes. Turn on the constellation lines and names (keys C and V, or the buttons on Stellarium Web’s bottom bar), and turn off the atmosphere (A) and the ground (G) when you need to see stars in daylight or below the horizon. Each object at each time is one row of the Data table: {#} rows in all.",
"<strong>Set up.</strong> Set the location to Ruston, Louisiana (Stellarium for Windows, Mac and Linux: the Location window, F6; Stellarium Web: click the place name on the bottom bar). If Ruston is not in the list, enter latitude {#}° N and longitude {#}° W. Set the date and time to January {#}, {#}, at {#}:{#} pm Central Standard Time (the Date/time window, F5; Stellarium Web: click the date and time at the bottom right). That is {#}:{#} UTC on January {#}; if your computer is in another time zone, convert.",
"<strong>Polaris.</strong> Record Polaris’s altitude, azimuth, right ascension and declination at {#}:{#} pm, then again at {#}:{#} am on January {#} (two rows).",
"<strong>Betelgeuse.</strong> Record the same four numbers for Betelgeuse, the bright red star in Orion, at {#}:{#} pm and again at {#}:{#} pm on January {#} (two rows).",
"<strong>Circumpolar or not.</strong> At {#}:{#} pm on January {#}, record only the altitude and declination (leave the azimuth and right ascension blank) for Dubhe (in the Big Dipper), Kochab (in the Little Dipper), Capella (in Auriga) and Vega (in Lyra), one row each. Then let time run forward {#} hours (Stellarium for Windows, Mac and Linux: press L until the stars move steadily, and K to go back to normal speed; Stellarium Web: in the date and time panel, move the hour forward two hours at a time) and write down which of the four ever reach an altitude below {#}°.",
"<strong>The Sun through the year.</strong> Record the Sun’s constellation, altitude, azimuth and declination at {#}:{#} UTC on each of these dates in {#}: January {#}, March {#}, June {#}, September {#} and December {#} (five rows). That is {#}:{#} noon Central time in January and December, and {#}:{#} pm in March, June and September, when Louisiana is on daylight saving time. Turn off the atmosphere to see the stars around the Sun. If the information does not name the Sun’s constellation, turn on the constellation boundaries (B) and see which one the Sun is inside.",
